# KNOWN GAP — a TRAILING comment binds to the FIRST element on its source line,
# not the element it follows.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture is an UNFORMATTED
# INPUT whose subject is what `gg fmt` does to it.
#
# The author wrote three elements on one line and a trailing comment after the
# LAST of them. The container has a magic comma but no break BETWEEN elements,
# so the engagement guard correctly returns it to the one-element-per-line shape
# — and the comment comes back attached to `1`, two lines above the `3` it
# annotates:
#
#     Vector[int] xs = [
#         1,    # annotates 3
#         2,
#         3,
#     ]
#
# MECHANISM: `emit_trailing_comment_after(elem_end, ..)` claims any comment on
# elem_end's SOURCE LINE. When several elements share that line, the first hook
# to run claims it, and the cursor is forward-only, so the elements that actually
# precede the comment never get a chance.
#
# INTENDED: the comment stays with the element it follows — `3,    # annotates 3`.
# A comment that silently changes which line it documents is the misattribution
# class the formatter's comment machinery exists to prevent.
#
# PRE-EXISTING, and measured byte-identical on the pre-change binary: this is NOT
# a regression from the author-line-grouping work. That feature makes the shape
# RARER (a preserved row keeps the comment beside the row it annotates) without
# touching the underlying binding.
#
# Wired as `#[ignore]`d `known_gap_fmt_trailing_comment_binds_to_first_on_line`
# in tests/integration.rs, asserting the INTENDED output. Un-ignore it in the
# commit that fixes the binding.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [
        1, 2, 3,  # annotates 3
    ]
    print(xs.len())
